VALUES ('d289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 'aws-saa-practice-2', 'AWS SAA-C03 Practice Paper 2', 'aws-saa', ARRAY['WAF', 'Secrets Manager', 'Route 53', 'DynamoDB', 'RDS Proxy', 'Auto Scaling', 'S3 Lifecycle', 'Compute Optimizer']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 2, 30);

-- ── Domain: Design Secure Architectures (30% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('fda444a2-579e-4759-b0a0-e1ba59c87a96', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 1, 'A company''s public-facing web application is being targeted by repeated SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS) attempts. Which AWS service should sit in front of the application to inspect and filter this type of malicious HTTP traffic?');
VALUES ('fda444a2-579e-4759-b0a0-e1ba59c87a96', 'c6939cad-3c03-454c-9dc8-e87a039cb6df', 'AWS WAF operates at Layer 7 and is purpose-built to inspect the actual content of HTTP requests — headers, body, query strings — against rules (AWS Managed Rules include ready-made SQL injection and XSS rule groups, or custom rules can be written) and block matching requests before they reach the application. AWS Shield protects against DDoS (volumetric/network-layer attacks), not content-based application-layer attacks like SQLi/XSS — Shield and WAF are complementary, not interchangeable. Both Security Groups and NACLs operate at Layer 3/4 (IP/port level) and have no visibility into HTTP request content at all.');

VALUES ('81aee77d-2042-4a83-bf77-718042754f9a', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 2, 'An application currently reads a database password from a hardcoded value in its configuration file. The company wants to store this credential securely, rotate it automatically on a schedule without any application code changes to fetch the new value''s location, and integrate directly with RDS for rotation. Which AWS service is the best fit?');
VALUES ('81aee77d-2042-4a83-bf77-718042754f9a', '7d1f9398-55c7-4c2b-9d47-3d56aa86f0d8', 'Secrets Manager is purpose-built for exactly this: it natively supports automatic, scheduled rotation of credentials and provides pre-built Lambda rotation function templates specifically for RDS (and other services), so the actual password value changes on schedule while the application always retrieves the current value via the same secret name/ARN — no code change needed to track a new location. Parameter Store (Standard tier) can store secrets but does not have this same built-in automatic-rotation-with-RDS integration (Parameter Store Advanced tier has some rotation support via custom automation, but Secrets Manager''s RDS integration is more direct and purpose-built). S3 versioning tracks object version history, not credential rotation. KMS manages encryption keys, not application secrets/passwords directly — it''s commonly used to encrypt secrets stored elsewhere (including in Secrets Manager itself), not as a secrets store on its own.');

VALUES ('0a2a5d05-793a-4913-8551-933bd2de5819', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 3, 'A security team needs to audit exactly which IP addresses and ports had traffic accepted or rejected at the network interface level for EC2 instances in a VPC, for forensic investigation after a suspected intrusion. Which AWS feature provides this?');
VALUES ('0a2a5d05-793a-4913-8551-933bd2de5819', '560d4ce1-b314-40e2-b7ef-d6f2b5cd315e', 'VPC Flow Logs capture IP traffic metadata (source/destination IP, source/destination port, protocol, bytes transferred, and whether the traffic was ACCEPTed or REJECTed by a Security Group or NACL) at the ENI, subnet, or VPC level — exactly the accept/reject-by-IP-and-port audit trail needed here. CloudTrail logs AWS API calls/management-plane actions (who created/modified/deleted a resource), not network packet-level traffic. AWS Config tracks resource configuration state and changes over time, not network traffic. Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability-scanning service (assessing EC2/container/Lambda for known vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure), not a traffic-logging service — none of the three distractors actually log traffic metadata the way Flow Logs do.');

VALUES ('3ebfb72f-fb4e-4f0b-aa22-06a3ebe32b38', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 4, 'An S3 bucket was accidentally made publicly readable due to a misconfigured bucket policy, exposing sensitive data. What is the single most direct AWS feature to prevent this class of mistake from happening again, account-wide, even if a future bucket policy or ACL is misconfigured?');
VALUES ('3ebfb72f-fb4e-4f0b-aa22-06a3ebe32b38', 'd8cc2bbf-4baa-45fe-95b6-b0cc04171efb', 'S3 Block Public Access is specifically designed as an override guardrail: once enabled (at the account level or per-bucket), it blocks public access even if a bucket policy or ACL is later misconfigured to grant it — this is precisely the "prevent this class of mistake account-wide, even against a future misconfiguration" requirement in the question, and AWS enables it by default on new accounts/buckets for exactly this reason. Versioning protects against accidental deletion/overwrite, not public exposure. Transfer Acceleration is a performance feature (faster uploads via CloudFront edge locations), unrelated to access control. Requester Pays shifts data-transfer cost to the requester but does not prevent public read access on its own — an object can still be public AND have Requester Pays enabled.');

VALUES ('42f590e3-bec9-481c-84af-fcdb4c451bac', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 5, 'An IAM policy needs to restrict a set of users so they can only access AWS resources when connecting from the company''s corporate office IP range. Which IAM policy feature achieves this?');
VALUES ('42f590e3-bec9-481c-84af-fcdb4c451bac', 'ac19a690-6761-49d5-b4f0-77ff09a03258', 'IAM policy Condition elements let a policy''s Allow or Deny apply only when a specified condition is true — the aws:SourceIp global condition key specifically evaluates the requester''s source IP address (supporting CIDR ranges), making it the correct mechanism to restrict access to a corporate office IP range directly within the IAM policy itself. The Resource element specifies which AWS resource(s) the policy applies to (an ARN), not network conditions. The Principal element specifies WHO the policy applies to (used in resource-based policies), not IP restriction. Security Groups can also restrict network-level access, but the question specifically asks about restricting IAM/API-level access by source IP, which is what the Condition/aws:SourceIp combination is built for.');

VALUES ('27b1c041-7a64-4071-bdc5-aff50e936003', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 6, 'A company wants baseline protection against common, large-scale DDoS attacks (e.g., SYN floods, UDP reflection) on their public-facing resources with no additional cost or configuration. What AWS feature already provides this by default?');
VALUES ('27b1c041-7a64-4071-bdc5-aff50e936003', 'ce01269d-0882-4f86-8bbd-57d40c56da5c', 'AWS Shield Standard is automatically active for every AWS customer at no additional cost, providing protection against the most common, frequently occurring network and transport layer (Layer 3/4) DDoS attacks (like SYN/UDP floods and reflection attacks) on resources such as CloudFront, Route 53, and ELB. Shield ADVANCED is the paid, opt-in tier that adds more sophisticated attack detection, near-real-time visibility, cost protection, and direct AWS DDoS Response Team (DRT) access — it is explicitly NOT free/automatic, which is the key distinction being tested. WAF requires deliberate configuration (rules must be created and associated) and is not "zero configuration."');

-- ── Domain: Design Resilient Architectures (26% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('7bb72be4-0f44-454d-8eab-c2a584d06649', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 7, 'An Auto Scaling Group needs to remain highly available even if an entire Availability Zone becomes unavailable. What is the correct configuration approach?');
VALUES ('7bb72be4-0f44-454d-8eab-c2a584d06649', 'fdf20197-60ee-4d1a-9446-aa304a399f94', 'An Auto Scaling Group is configured to span multiple AZs by associating it with subnets in more than one AZ — the ASG then distributes launched instances across those AZs (and, with an ELB attached, the load balancer routes traffic to healthy instances regardless of which AZ they''re in). This means an entire AZ failure only removes a portion of capacity, and the ASG can launch replacement instances in the remaining healthy AZ(s) to restore desired capacity. ASGs are Regional constructs that span AZs within that Region — they are NOT automatically multi-Region, and a single-AZ configuration is exactly the anti-pattern that creates the failure risk described in the question. Splitting into separate per-AZ ASGs with manual Route 53 weighted routing is a real but far more operationally complex pattern than simply configuring one ASG across multiple subnets/AZs behind a single ELB.');

VALUES ('0fcda11d-d020-41a9-a5a0-d5b67380bcdc', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 8, 'A solutions architect is explaining S3''s "eleven nines" (99.999999999%) figure to a stakeholder who assumes it means the S3 service itself is guaranteed to be reachable 99.999999999% of the time. What is the correct clarification?');
VALUES ('0fcda11d-d020-41a9-a5a0-d5b67380bcdc', 'da17b8ce-dcfa-4de7-a4f9-cac9125fb0aa', 'Durability and availability are genuinely distinct S3 concepts and a frequently tested SAA distinction: durability (99.999999999%, "eleven nines") describes how unlikely S3 is to actually lose your data once stored — a function of how many copies are stored redundantly across multiple facilities. Availability describes what percentage of the time the service is actually operational and able to serve requests, which is a separate, lower figure specified in S3''s own published SLA (S3 Standard targets 99.99% availability) — a much more achievable number than eleven nines, and the two should never be conflated. It is a real, backed technical distinction with published SLAs, not just marketing, and has nothing to do with encryption.');

VALUES ('10481296-c07d-4df4-a25e-3d26e408136b', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 9, 'A company runs a primary application in the us-east-1 Region and wants to automatically fail over DNS resolution to a standby deployment in us-west-2 if the primary becomes unhealthy. Which Route 53 feature combination achieves this?');
VALUES ('10481296-c07d-4df4-a25e-3d26e408136b', 'b7133761-f50e-4c01-af81-1f8d9a6f144a', 'Route 53 Health Checks actively monitor an endpoint''s health (via HTTP/HTTPS/TCP checks), and the Failover routing policy uses that health check status to automatically route DNS queries to a designated primary record when healthy, and to the secondary record when the primary is marked unhealthy — exactly the automatic cross-region failover behavior described. Simple routing policy has no health-check awareness at all — it just returns a fixed set of values with no failover logic. Geolocation routing routes based on the requester''s geographic location, not endpoint health, and is not the failover-specific policy type. Route 53 absolutely supports this natively; no third-party DNS provider is required.');

VALUES ('a8ae9f66-8b04-467c-a2f0-e7dac8f93d97', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 10, 'A microservice occasionally experiences traffic spikes that overwhelm a downstream processing service, causing failed requests. The upstream and downstream services do not need to communicate synchronously. Which AWS service, inserted between them, would improve resilience by absorbing the spike and decoupling the two services?');
VALUES ('a8ae9f66-8b04-467c-a2f0-e7dac8f93d97', '6bcc341c-2121-41cc-b73d-23d9553d3389', 'SQS is the classic decoupling pattern for exactly this scenario: the upstream service pushes messages onto a queue instead of calling the downstream service directly, and the downstream service pulls and processes messages at whatever rate it can sustainably handle — a traffic spike fills the queue rather than overwhelming or crashing the downstream service, directly improving resilience through asynchronous buffering. CloudFront is a CDN for content delivery to end users, not a service-to-service decoupling mechanism. Direct Connect is a dedicated network link to AWS, unrelated to buffering or scaling. Certificate Manager issues/manages TLS certificates and has nothing to do with resilience against traffic spikes.');

VALUES ('5931453a-ca32-4c68-b536-132f9daf70da', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 11, 'A company needs point-in-time recovery for their RDS database, allowing restoration to any specific second within the last 7 days, in addition to daily backups. What RDS capability provides this?');
VALUES ('5931453a-ca32-4c68-b536-132f9daf70da', '7b8bf638-4cea-430d-a5cd-476144afe197', 'RDS automated backups are the feature that specifically enables point-in-time recovery: RDS takes a daily full snapshot during the configured backup window AND continuously captures transaction logs throughout the day, so a restore operation can replay logs up to (and stop at) any specific second within the retention window (configurable up to 35 days) — reconstructing the database exactly as it existed at that moment, not just at the last daily snapshot. Manual snapshots alone (option A''s premise) only restore to the exact snapshot moment, which is why automated backups'' continuous log capture is the actual answer. Read Replicas serve read traffic and provide no backup/point-in-time-restore capability of their own. RDS absolutely supports point-in-time recovery via automated backups — this is a core, frequently-tested RDS feature.');

-- ── Domain: Design High-Performing Architectures (24% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('4d0af331-9c20-4039-a3f2-7175db9b7ef8', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 12, 'A DynamoDB table uses a customer''s signup date as its partition key. As the table grows, most read/write traffic concentrates on the most recent dates, causing throttling on those specific partitions while older partitions sit nearly idle. What is the root cause and the general fix?');
VALUES ('4d0af331-9c20-4039-a3f2-7175db9b7ef8', '9a640304-6775-4f77-a1fc-5c0da8ef966c', 'DynamoDB distributes data (and the request throughput budget) across partitions based on the partition key''s hash — a partition key like "signup date" has low cardinality (relatively few distinct values) and is naturally time-clustered, so a large share of real-world traffic concentrates on whichever date-partitions are currently "hot" (most recent), overwhelming those specific partitions'' individual throughput allocation even if the table''s AGGREGATE provisioned capacity is more than sufficient overall — simply adding more total capacity doesn''t fix an uneven distribution problem. The standard fix is picking a higher-cardinality key (e.g., a user ID) or adding a calculated/random suffix to spread the same logical data across many more distinct partition key values, distributing load evenly. This is a well-known, frequently-tested DynamoDB design pattern, not a reason to abandon DynamoDB entirely.');

VALUES ('6d7d0081-65c5-4cf8-a122-7f96bdaeeef0', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 13, 'A company runs a real-time multiplayer gaming backend using UDP and TCP, requiring the lowest possible latency and consistent routing over the AWS global network for users worldwide — not caching of static content. Which service is the better fit: CloudFront or Global Accelerator?');
VALUES ('6d7d0081-65c5-4cf8-a122-7f96bdaeeef0', 'f574dff5-f71e-4bef-afe7-60ee3d88758f', 'Global Accelerator is designed for exactly this profile: it improves performance for a broader range of traffic (TCP and UDP, not just HTTP/HTTPS) by using static anycast IP addresses and routing over the AWS global backbone network to the optimal, healthy endpoint — well suited to real-time gaming, VoIP, and IoT workloads where content caching is irrelevant and low, consistent latency for non-HTTP protocols is the actual goal. CloudFront is a content-delivery/caching CDN specifically for HTTP(S) content (web assets, video, APIs) — it does not accelerate raw UDP game traffic and caching isn''t applicable to a real-time multiplayer stream. The two are genuinely different services solving different problems, often used together in different parts of an architecture, not interchangeable or identical.');

VALUES ('e895d1ca-7ff1-4da4-a04b-d4cf3d66311a', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 14, 'A serverless application using AWS Lambda experiences database connection exhaustion on its RDS instance during traffic spikes, because each concurrent Lambda invocation opens its own new database connection and RDS has a hard connection limit. What AWS feature is designed specifically to solve this?');
VALUES ('e895d1ca-7ff1-4da4-a04b-d4cf3d66311a', 'f0e9e785-3c66-44db-bdac-dbbb8d1b90f2', 'RDS Proxy is a fully managed database proxy purpose-built for exactly this Lambda-plus-RDS connection-exhaustion problem: it maintains a pool of actual database connections and multiplexes many concurrent Lambda invocations'' logical connections over that smaller, managed pool, instead of every invocation opening (and RDS having to accept) a brand-new physical connection — directly preventing the connection-limit exhaustion described. Multi-AZ adds a standby for failover/HA, not additional usable connection capacity during normal operation (the standby isn''t serving application traffic). X-Ray provides distributed tracing/observability and has no effect on connection pooling. Lambda-to-RDS is a well-supported, common architecture — RDS Proxy exists specifically because it''s common enough to need this dedicated solution.');

VALUES ('3bf81f0e-f6db-42b4-89d2-cd37ce406a0c', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 15, 'A company needs a shared file system that multiple EC2 instances across several Availability Zones can mount and read/write to concurrently, with POSIX file semantics (not object storage). Which AWS storage service fits this requirement?');
VALUES ('3bf81f0e-f6db-42b4-89d2-cd37ce406a0c', '75ae38b0-b0d9-49f8-aa22-eb1e0ec0a35d', 'EFS is a managed, scalable NFS (Network File System) that natively supports being mounted concurrently by thousands of EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones, with standard POSIX file permissions and semantics — exactly matching this shared-file, cross-AZ, POSIX requirement. EBS volumes, in the standard (non-io2-Multi-Attach) case, attach to only a single EC2 instance at a time (io2 Multi-Attach is a narrow exception limited to instances in the same AZ, not the general case, and still isn''t a full shared file system with POSIX semantics across many instances) — EBS is fundamentally block storage for one instance, not concurrent shared file access. S3 is object storage accessed via HTTP API calls, not a mountable POSIX file system, despite tools existing to approximate file-system-like access on top of it.');

VALUES ('b85aa220-506c-489b-9282-43424e0bc66a', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 16, 'A DynamoDB-backed application requires microsecond (not just millisecond) read latency for its most frequently accessed items, significantly faster than DynamoDB alone typically provides. Which AWS service is purpose-built as an in-memory cache specifically for DynamoDB, requiring minimal application code changes?');
VALUES ('b85aa220-506c-489b-9282-43424e0bc66a', '1f9854b2-71dc-4637-95b4-9311f7d6350b', 'DAX (DynamoDB Accelerator) is a DynamoDB-specific, fully managed, write-through in-memory cache that sits directly in front of a DynamoDB table, is API-compatible with the DynamoDB SDK (requiring minimal code changes — often just swapping the client), and reduces read latency from single-digit milliseconds down to microseconds for cached items. ElastiCache (Redis/Memcached) is a general-purpose cache that CAN be used in front of any database including DynamoDB, but requires the application to explicitly manage cache read/write logic itself and is not purpose-built or API-compatible with DynamoDB the way DAX is — this is a genuine, testable distinction, not interchangeable tools. Global Accelerator improves network routing performance, it does not cache database query results at all.');

-- ── Domain: Design Cost-Optimized Architectures (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('2521a3e6-c5ae-4993-8d25-d1f9d7fd37bc', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 17, 'A company has S3 objects that are frequently accessed for the first 30 days after creation, rarely accessed for the next 60 days, and almost never accessed after that (but must be retained for compliance for 7 years). They want this transition automated without manual intervention. What is the correct approach?');
VALUES ('2521a3e6-c5ae-4993-8d25-d1f9d7fd37bc', '4d4479a1-6c3e-4fba-839e-62babd2dc06d', 'S3 Lifecycle policies are the built-in mechanism for exactly this predictable, age-based transition pattern — rules can be configured to automatically transition objects to a cheaper storage class after a defined number of days (Standard → Standard-IA → Glacier → Glacier Deep Archive), and to expire (delete) objects after a defined retention period, all without any manual intervention or custom automation code, based purely on object age since creation. This differs from S3 Intelligent-Tiering, which reacts to actual observed access patterns rather than following a fixed, predictable age-based schedule — for a KNOWN, predictable access pattern like this one (fixed 30/90-day thresholds), Lifecycle rules are the more direct, standard answer. CloudFront is a content delivery/caching service, not a storage-class management or archival tool.');

VALUES ('38475de3-101e-4b4c-8176-b31eacf2df11', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 18, 'A company suspects several of their EC2 instances are significantly over-provisioned (low CPU/memory utilization relative to instance size) but does not want to manually analyze CloudWatch metrics for every instance individually. Which AWS service analyzes historical utilization and provides specific instance right-sizing recommendations?');
VALUES ('38475de3-101e-4b4c-8176-b31eacf2df11', '88e8cdc9-6375-4ee0-9c1f-6dca218a56b8', 'AWS Compute Optimizer is purpose-built for this: it analyzes historical CloudWatch utilization data (CPU, memory with the CloudWatch agent installed, network, EBS) across EC2 instances, Auto Scaling Groups, EBS volumes, and Lambda functions, and produces specific, actionable recommendations — such as "this instance is over-provisioned, consider downsizing to instance type X" — at no additional cost, without requiring manual per-instance metric analysis. Direct Connect is a dedicated network connectivity service, unrelated to instance sizing. Certificate Manager issues/manages TLS certificates, also unrelated. This is a real, first-party AWS capability specifically because manually right-sizing a large fleet by hand doesn''t scale.');

VALUES ('f6fa1991-067d-4f57-876a-208a4031ee0e', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 19, 'Two EC2 instances that transfer large volumes of data between each other are currently deployed in two different Availability Zones within the same Region. What is the most direct way to reduce the data transfer cost between them, assuming no other constraint prevents it?');
VALUES ('f6fa1991-067d-4f57-876a-208a4031ee0e', '0f198fd1-7f7c-49d7-96f8-303f1de4b333', 'AWS data transfer pricing genuinely differs by scope: transfer between instances in the SAME Availability Zone (especially within the same placement group/using private IPs) is free or lowest-cost, transfer between different AZs within the same Region carries a per-GB charge in both directions, and transfer across Regions or to the internet costs more still. Consolidating tightly-coupled, high-volume-transfer instances into the same AZ (trading away some AZ-level redundancy for this specific pair, a real trade-off to weigh) is the direct cost lever here. S3 Transfer Acceleration is unrelated (it''s for accelerating uploads to S3 over long distances, not EC2-to-EC2 transfer). IPv6 vs IPv4 addressing does not itself change AWS''s data transfer pricing structure — that''s a routing/addressing choice, not a cost lever.');

VALUES ('00a604ff-00bf-4438-ba05-0037525c3513', 'd289b99e-2765-4074-8f63-9cb49323cc79', 20, 'A company allocated several Elastic IP addresses for EC2 instances that have since been terminated, leaving the Elastic IPs unattached. A cost review later flags unexpectedly high networking charges. What is the correct explanation?');
VALUES ('00a604ff-00bf-4438-ba05-0037525c3513', 'f1f2aebf-91ad-47d1-8a3d-18f2adc6bf95', 'This is a genuinely counter-intuitive, frequently-tested AWS billing detail, and exactly the opposite of what most people assume: an Elastic IP address attached to a RUNNING instance is free, but AWS charges an hourly rate for an Elastic IP that is allocated to an account but NOT attached to a running instance (including one attached to a STOPPED instance) — the logic being that idle IPv4 addresses are a scarce resource AWS wants to discourage hoarding. This is a classic real-world cost leak: terminating an instance without also releasing its associated Elastic IP leaves it silently accruing hourly charges indefinitely, exactly matching the scenario described. This is a real, current, documented AWS pricing behavior, not a misconception to dismiss.');
